{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787060613,"nonce":"hIEpv6GnBUaeIz5Ku8jfGXPnL8w=","title":"A flapping alert opens one case per flap, so the only place left to reduce the noise is the notification layer","message":"**What is wrong.** A case opens when an alert starts firing and closes when it resolves. An alert that\nresolves and re-fires six times in ten minutes therefore produces six cases, six thread replies and six\npings — and because a case is strictly terminal since `00054`, nothing merges them. The product's answer\ntoday is to dampen at the notification layer (`notifications.suppressed_reason = 'flapping'`, and a digest\ninstead of individual messages), which hides the noise rather than not making it.\n\n**The demonstration.** `alerts.flap_score` and `alerts.is_flapping` already detect this\n(`db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:49-50`), and `internal/notification/domain/broadcast_test.go:160` asserts\n\"a flapping alert produces a digest\" — the mechanism exists precisely because the case stream is noisy.\n`alerts.is_flapping`'s own column comment states the intent the current design cannot honour: \"A VISIBLE UI\nstate, never silent suppression -- silence destroys trust (SPEC §B.6)\".\n\n**Why it matters.** Damping at delivery makes a suppressed notification indistinguishable from a signal\nthat never fired, which is the one thing an alerting product cannot afford. Shaping the case instead means\nthe noise never exists: one case across the flap, one notification, one thread reply, and an operator who\ncan still see `is_flapping` and go fix the Prometheus rule that is missing a `for:`.\n\n**Where.** `internal/alerts/domain/case.go` (the close transition) · `internal/alerts/service/sweep.go`\n(T6/expire) · `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:49-50` · `internal/tuning` (the new policy) ·\n`internal/notification/domain/broadcast.go` · `docs/adr/0041-*.md` (the Case definition to restate).\n\n**Scope.** large\n\n**Done when.**\n1. A `case_policy_config` exists, keyed on `namespace` × `alertname` — the same axes the group key already\n   uses (ADR 0038), so an operator learns one set of dimensions and not two.\n2. It carries a retention window W: a case whose alert has resolved stays OPEN for W, and closes only once\n   the alert has stayed resolved for W. A re-fire inside W finds the case still open.\n3. A case is still strictly terminal — this is a DELAYED CLOSE, never a reopen. It closes exactly once.\n   A test asserts a resolve/re-fire/resolve sequence inside W yields ONE case with one `started_at`.\n4. W defaults to 0, and W=0 is byte-for-byte today's behaviour. A test asserts that.\n5. ADR 0041 restates the Case: not \"open until resolved\" but \"open until resolved and stayed resolved for W\",\n   with W=0 as the degenerate case.\n6. `notifications.suppressed_reason` no longer admits `flapping`, and the flapping digest is deleted —\n   retention makes it unnecessary. `is_flapping` remains visible.","files":null}]}